diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/apple/ave/tests/README b/drivers/media/platform/apple/ave/tests/README
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..ea59e9724e
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/apple/ave/tests/README
@@ -0,0 +1,146 @@
+AVE host tests
+==============
+
+Run python3 run.py with a C compiler providing ASan and UBSan (CC may name
+its executable). No encoder, firmware or captured command image is needed.
+
+run.py reads the real AVE structs and compiles the unmodified bodies of
+ave-image.c, ave-layout.c, ave-abi.c, ave-writer.c and ave-cmd.c, plus the real
+buffer-allocation,
+free, reply and firmware-build-gate functions. Only includes and unused
+kernel-facing declarations are omitted. shim.h supplies host primitives;
+boundary.h replaces IPC, DMA and logging. This does not validate the kernel
+API integration, DART mappings, boot sequence or output extraction.
+
+--compare-baseline PATH builds an older AVE source directory and compares
+all bytes of 288 synthetic sessions (1,440 Start/Process commands, including
+headers, addresses and zero padding). It covers both codecs, geometry,
+profiles, B-frame settings, rates, CBR/VBR and forced-key-frame fields.
+
+--build PATH retains the executable for offline capture QA. Its --case
+interface emits synthetic commands; capture inputs and generated artifacts
+must stay outside the public source tree. Compare only with the matching
+firmware layout, mask enumerated pointer fields and host-only headers, and
+report inherited differences explicitly. The 26A428 description is not
+reachable through the runtime CRC lookup.
+
+The host shim intentionally uses stdint types, Linux's private ENOTSUPP
+value and side-effect-free calls to kernel-like arithmetic macros. Those
+produce checkpatch PREFER_KERNEL_TYPES, ENOTSUPP and MACRO_ARG_REUSE notices.
+MAINTAINERS already covers the entire AVE directory (FILE_PATH_CHANGES).
+
+The ABI refactor also has in-kernel arm64 KUnit tests selected by
+VIDEO_APPLE_AVE_CMD_KUNIT_TEST. The host runner understands both the older
+single-builder sources and the per-ABI writer split for baseline comparison.
+
+Recovery and PM checks
+----------------------
+
+Run python3 run-recovery.py for ASan/UBSan checks of the production recovery,
+firmware shutdown, suspend/resume, failed-session cleanup, queue and IPC pump
+callbacks. It covers two owners (one idle), both ready queues plus an owned
+OUTPUT buffer, racing QBUF, PM reference balance, DMA quarantine/reclamation,
+retained power, failed control stop, the recovery limit, pristine DATA restore
+failure, suspend refusal/rollback and a busy IPC stream reaching its deadline.
+MMIO, DMA allocation and PM are simulated: this cannot establish hardware reset
+values or that a VENC domain actually powered down.
+
+The recovery runner also uses the production OUTPUT memory operations and
+Process submission callback. Both MMAP and imported dma-buf mappings survive
+immediate vb2 release after ERROR, including loss of the importing fd and an
+uncertain command submission. The fake DMA boundary rejects any release of a
+command-referenced mapping before the control-off marker AND simulated shared
+DART/VENC power loss. A failed stop and CTRL=0 with retained BOOT_ARG must not
+reclaim it. Allocator failures and ordinary import/map/release are covered too.
+
+The apple-ave-recovery KUnit suite tests the actual sliding-window helper at
+expiry boundaries, with staggered attempts and across jiffies wrap. Run with
+../.kunitconfig using tools/testing/kunit/kunit.py. The existing
+tools/asahi/ave-pm-tests.py also checks the #651 sleep rollback contract.
+
+Hardware procedure (requires a qualified firmware/SoC and an approved lane)
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+1. Record the kernel revision, firmware CRCs and boot ID. Mount debugfs and
+   enable the normal PM/genpd tracing available in the test kernel. Confirm a
+   fresh AVC and HEVC encode can each complete and decode correctly.
+2. Start a long encode with several OUTPUT and CAPTURE buffers queued. While
+   it runs, as root write 1 to /sys/kernel/debug/apple-ave/inject_wedge.
+   This software fault marks the current firmware broken and suppresses the
+   recovery Halt attempt, modelling a command timeout without malformed DMA
+   commands. It does not reproduce a real DART overrun or a firmware deadlock.
+3. Check that the client promptly sees POLLERR/EIO and every buffer still owned
+   by the driver completes with ERROR, including OUTPUT slots already handed
+   to firmware. A V4L2 queue exerciser should count completions; ffmpeg alone
+   only checks the client-visible exit. QBUF after failure must fail or return
+   the buffer with ERROR. STREAMOFF both queues before restarting that context.
+4. Immediately start a new encode without rebooting. Allow the existing bounded
+   PM retry loop for DARTs sharing VENC power. Require the recovery stop log,
+   a real domain power-off/on in PM tracing, the power-cycle-confirmed log,
+   fresh firmware Config and valid encoded output. The driver re-arms BOOT_ARG
+   with control off and requires it to read zero after resume; qualify that
+   reset witness on each target. A retained nonzero witness must cause EAGAIN,
+   with no DATA restore, allocation reuse or new firmware start.
+5. Repeat with two concurrent clients, including an idle open session with no
+   outstanding frames. Both must fail, all PM references must drop, and a new
+   client must encode. Check there are no stale replies, DART faults or DMA
+   allocation growth over repeated recoveries.
+6. Inject three faults inside ten minutes. A fourth must report the recovery
+   limit and return queue errors without starting another firmware boot. After
+   the oldest attempt expires, a new session must recover successfully.
+7. In a separate approved sleep test, check normal encode/sleep/resume and a
+   halt-failure suspend refusal. A failed suspend must restart the m2m scheduler
+   (#651); system sleep must never treat the forced runtime stop as a successful
+   firmware Halt. Preserve logs from failed sleep attempts as well as retries.
+
+Run this procedure on each target requiring qualification, including both
+J616s and Neo. Repeat the recovery with a genuine firmware timeout/DART fault
+when a safe reproducer is available. Keep captures and machine records outside
+the public source tree. Rollback is a normal revert of the recovery commits;
+use the lane's existing boot fallback when testing candidate kernels.
+
+LEAD-RUN: Neo failed-session mapping regression
+----------------------------------------------
+
+Run in the approved Neo hardware lane, using the hardware procedure above.
+This is a re-test plan, not a claim of hardware qualification. Preserve the
+normal candidate-kernel boot fallback and the known-good module/kernel for
+rollback. Record the full source commit, kernel config, module hash/vermagic,
+firmware CRCs and boot ID with the results. Build and install the matching
+candidate through the lane's normal procedure; do not load a module built for
+a different kernel. Keep machine journals and captures outside public source.
+
+1. Save a complete kernel log before the test and collect a continuous kernel
+   journal with monotonic timestamps throughout each scenario. Establish fresh
+   AVC and HEVC encode/decode success. Use the same queue exerciser as the
+   original hardware procedure to record injection time, first POLLERR/EIO and
+   each buffer's completion count/state. Include MMAP OUTPUT and imported
+   dma-buf OUTPUT runs, with immediate DQBUF, STREAMOFF, REQBUFS(0), close and
+   release of the importing fd after the error. This exercises lifetime beyond
+   the V4L2 context. Do not require the client to wait for the stop or reset.
+2. Re-run hardware steps 2-4: inject during a long encode, require prompt queue
+   errors (compare with the prior 5-9 ms POLLERR measurements), and exactly one
+   ERROR completion per buffer still owned by the driver. Require the existing
+   recovery stop and power-cycle-confirmed messages and a valid new encode
+   without rebooting. Inspect the entire interval from injection through the
+   subsequent encode, not just the recovery's last messages.
+3. Re-run step 5 with two clients, including the idle owner. Release both
+   contexts promptly after ERROR. Require both clients to fail promptly,
+   balanced PM ownership, a confirmed recovery and a valid fresh encode.
+4. Re-run step 6 with a fresh recovery budget: three injections in ten minutes,
+   restarting and verifying an encode after each. The next failure must retain
+   queue errors and report the three-attempt limit without a new boot. After
+   the oldest attempt expires, require successful recovery and encoding. Use
+   the lane's normal fresh-boot procedure or allow the earlier attempts to
+   expire between scenarios; do not reset the counter by reloading the driver.
+5. PASS requires zero apple-dart translation faults after EVERY injection,
+   including the limited attempt and its expiry/retry, with no new suppressed
+   fault messages. Preserve complete logs and per-injection windows, completion
+   counts, error latency and recovery results even on failure. Any new DART
+   fault, missing/duplicate completion, lost prompt error or unconfirmed reset
+   is a failed re-test. The existing strict sleep check in step 7 remains
+   required in its separate approved sleep lane.
+
+On failure, preserve the candidate logs and restore the known-good kernel or
+module through the lane's rollback procedure. Keep the draft unmerged pending
+this LEAD-RUN evidence. Software injection does not qualify a real DMA overrun.
